WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
One champion dairy bull can father tens of

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.480
thousands of calves, across many countries, without ever

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.640
meeting a cow.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.560
A wheat breeder crosses her chosen plants one

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.000
ear at a time.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.800
Both are choosing which parents' gametes meet.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.120
So how do artificial insemination and artificial pollination

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.720
compare, in their steps and in their outcomes?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.560
And why can one of them reshape a

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.640
whole breed so much faster?

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:29.946
First, what they share.

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:33.786
Both choose which parents reproduce, but neither changes

00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:35.066
any DNA.

00:00:35.307 --> 00:00:37.466
And the parents' gametes still vary.

00:00:37.947 --> 00:00:41.146
Meiosis gives each sperm, and each pollen grain,

00:00:41.227 --> 00:00:43.706
its own mix of the parent's alleles, so

00:00:43.787 --> 00:00:45.626
the offspring still differ.

00:00:45.947 --> 00:00:48.186
Even sex-sorted semen isn't certain.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.706
Sperm carrying an X chromosome have slightly more

00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:54.586
DNA, so a machine can sort them, but

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.306
not perfectly: it raises the chance of a

00:00:57.507 --> 00:01:00.186
female calf to about nine in ten.

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.986
These methods make an outcome more likely, not

00:01:03.027 --> 00:01:03.626
certain.

00:01:04.834 --> 00:01:07.633
Now compare the processes, one step at a

00:01:07.714 --> 00:01:08.193
time.

00:01:08.754 --> 00:01:11.073
Step one, collect and store.

00:01:11.714 --> 00:01:14.833
Semen is collected from the chosen bull, checked,

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.793
diluted into hundreds of doses and frozen in

00:01:17.874 --> 00:01:20.113
thin straws in liquid nitrogen.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:23.073
Stored like this, it keeps for decades and

00:01:23.154 --> 00:01:24.553
can be sent anywhere.

00:01:24.914 --> 00:01:26.833
Pollen is collected from the anthers of the

00:01:26.914 --> 00:01:28.033
chosen plant.

00:01:28.274 --> 00:01:30.513
But the pollen of many crops survives only

00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:33.873
hours unless it's specially dried and frozen, so

00:01:33.954 --> 00:01:36.433
it's usually used fresh, close by.

00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:40.993
Step two, deliver the gametes, and keep every

00:01:41.074 --> 00:01:42.513
other source out.

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:45.793
For the cow, timing is the control: a

00:01:45.874 --> 00:01:48.033
worker thaws a dose and places it in

00:01:48.114 --> 00:01:50.273
her uterus while she's on heat, and no

00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:52.753
bull is there, so no other sperm can

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:53.393
arrive.

00:01:54.034 --> 00:01:56.593
A flower is harder, because pollen can come

00:01:56.674 --> 00:01:59.953
from two other places: its own anthers, and

00:02:00.114 --> 00:02:01.553
the wind or insects.

00:02:02.114 --> 00:02:04.353
So the breeder must remove the seed flower's

00:02:04.434 --> 00:02:07.393
anthers before they shed pollen, protect it from

00:02:07.474 --> 00:02:10.433
stray pollen until the chosen pollen is applied,

00:02:10.514 --> 00:02:12.593
and keep other pollen out while the cross

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:13.433
is made.

00:02:13.714 --> 00:02:15.473
A bag is one way to isolate the

00:02:15.554 --> 00:02:16.113
flower.

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:18.813
Now the outcomes.

00:02:19.094 --> 00:02:21.253
Both give offspring from the parents the breeder

00:02:21.334 --> 00:02:22.133
chose.

00:02:22.374 --> 00:02:24.653
The big difference is reach.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:28.373
Because frozen semen keeps and travels, one bull's

00:02:28.494 --> 00:02:31.013
alleles can reach tens of thousands of calves

00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:32.373
in many countries.

00:02:33.014 --> 00:02:36.453
Hand pollination usually works flower by flower, so

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:38.973
one plant's reach is much smaller.

00:02:39.414 --> 00:02:42.933
Seed companies control pollination across whole fields, though.

00:02:43.494 --> 00:02:45.733
To make hybrid maize seed, a cross of

00:02:45.894 --> 00:02:49.173
two chosen parent lines, the pollen-making tassels are

00:02:49.454 --> 00:02:51.973
removed from the seed rows, and wind carries

00:02:52.054 --> 00:02:54.213
pollen from the chosen rows beside them.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.513
That reach changes the gene pool.

00:02:58.154 --> 00:03:01.513
Picture ten thousand cows, all inseminated with semen

00:03:01.594 --> 00:03:03.113
from just five bulls.

00:03:03.674 --> 00:03:06.473
Every calf has one of five fathers, so

00:03:06.514 --> 00:03:09.513
the herd behaves genetically like a small population.

00:03:10.154 --> 00:03:13.353
Chance loses alleles faster, the animals become more

00:03:13.434 --> 00:03:16.873
closely related, and a harmful recessive allele carried

00:03:16.954 --> 00:03:19.353
by a popular bull becomes common enough to

00:03:19.474 --> 00:03:20.553
pair up in calves.

00:03:21.274 --> 00:03:24.473
In Holstein cattle, several inherited disorders have been

00:03:24.514 --> 00:03:27.113
traced back to single bulls whose semen was

00:03:27.194 --> 00:03:28.553
used very widely.

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:32.086
So the narrowing comes from how widely a

00:03:32.247 --> 00:03:35.526
few parents are used, not from insemination itself.

00:03:36.087 --> 00:03:39.286
Using semen from many unrelated bulls keeps more

00:03:39.447 --> 00:03:42.406
alleles, and importing it from distant herds brings

00:03:42.487 --> 00:03:43.286
new ones in.

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:46.326
That's gene flow, so the same method can

00:03:46.487 --> 00:03:47.846
widen a gene pool.

00:03:48.087 --> 00:03:49.606
And it isn't only fathers.

00:03:50.007 --> 00:03:52.966
Embryo transfer lets one prize cow have many

00:03:53.047 --> 00:03:55.926
calves, carried by other cows, so leaning on

00:03:56.007 --> 00:03:58.366
a few mothers narrows a breed too.

00:03:58.647 --> 00:04:01.206
In crops, too, it's the choice that matters.

00:04:01.687 --> 00:04:05.246
Crossing two varieties makes new combinations of alleles.

00:04:05.447 --> 00:04:08.326
The narrowing comes later, if one new variety

00:04:08.407 --> 00:04:09.686
is planted everywhere.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.860
In an exam, compare means dealing with the

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.860
same feature on both sides.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:18.500
So don't write a paragraph on each method.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.580
Take one feature at a time.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.940
Both choose the parents, but semen is frozen

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.060
and sent anywhere, whereas pollen is mostly used

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:27.620
fresh.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.980
A cow's timing keeps other sperm out, whereas

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.620
a flower needs its anthers removed and stray

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:34.940
pollen kept off.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.100
And insemination's reach can narrow a breed far

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:38.900
faster.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.900
Two things to hold on to.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.100
Same aim, different reach: both choose the parents,

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.740
but frozen semen can spread one male much

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:49.300
further.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.580
And to judge a breed's genetic health, count

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.660
the fathers, not the cows.

00:04:55.867 --> 00:04:57.306
Two exam questions now.

00:04:57.947 --> 00:04:58.666
First.

00:04:58.987 --> 00:05:01.866
A tomato breeder wants seeds whose father is

00:05:01.947 --> 00:05:03.706
one chosen tomato plant.

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.386
Tomato flowers usually pollinate themselves.

00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:10.426
A cattle breeder wants calves whose father is

00:05:10.507 --> 00:05:12.026
a bull in another state.

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:15.386
Compare the processes each breeder would use to

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:18.186
make sure only the chosen father's gametes take

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:18.666
part.

00:05:19.387 --> 00:05:20.666
Pause here to answer.

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:33.826
Both collect gametes from the chosen father.

00:05:34.107 --> 00:05:36.106
The bull's semen can be frozen and sent

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:39.306
interstate, whereas tomato pollen comes from a plant

00:05:39.387 --> 00:05:40.106
close by.

00:05:40.747 --> 00:05:43.146
The cow is inseminated on heat with no

00:05:43.227 --> 00:05:46.426
bull present, whereas each tomato flower needs its

00:05:46.507 --> 00:05:49.546
anthers removed before they shed, and stray pollen

00:05:49.627 --> 00:05:52.346
kept off until the chosen pollen goes on,

00:05:52.387 --> 00:05:54.026
a bag being one option.

00:05:54.347 --> 00:05:57.946
Describing each breeder separately is the classic mistake.

00:05:59.167 --> 00:05:59.726
Second.

00:06:00.207 --> 00:06:02.446
In a dairy breed, the number of cows

00:06:02.527 --> 00:06:05.086
has stayed about the same for forty years.

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:08.126
Over that time, the number of bulls fathering

00:06:08.207 --> 00:06:11.326
calves fell sharply, and more calves were born

00:06:11.407 --> 00:06:13.406
with an inherited recessive disorder.

00:06:14.047 --> 00:06:17.806
Analyse how artificial insemination could explain these data,

00:06:17.887 --> 00:06:19.406
and suggest one change.

00:06:19.967 --> 00:06:21.246
Pause here to answer.

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:35.266
With fewer bulls, each fathers far more calves,

00:06:35.347 --> 00:06:38.066
so the breed behaves like a small population,

00:06:38.187 --> 00:06:39.746
though cow numbers held steady.

00:06:40.387 --> 00:06:43.506
Animals grow more related, so recessive alleles from

00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:45.986
popular bulls pair up more often, and the

00:06:46.107 --> 00:06:47.266
disorder rises.

00:06:47.987 --> 00:06:52.546
Change: use more unrelated bulls, or DNA-test them

00:06:52.627 --> 00:06:54.466
and avoid mating two carriers.

00:06:55.107 --> 00:06:57.586
Thinking the same number of cows keeps diversity

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.746
safe is the classic mistake.

00:07:00.967 --> 00:07:04.726
So to compare reproductive technologies, take one feature

00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:07.126
at a time: how the gametes are collected

00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:09.766
and stored, how they're delivered and how other

00:07:09.847 --> 00:07:12.726
gametes are kept out, and the outcomes, for

00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:14.806
the offspring and for the gene pool.

00:07:15.687 --> 00:07:20.086
Next, copying instead of choosing: how cloning works.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.740
Here are the key notes.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.900
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:25.780
to copy them.
